message.content:
{
  "summary": "Surface runoff is generated by gravity-driven flow over land when precipitation exceeds infiltration capacity or soil saturation, moving toward channels within a watershed where lakes, wetlands, and snow/ice act as temporary storage. Water movement operates across varying timescales without a single compulsory loop, quantified by water budgets that track inputs, outputs, and storage changes; human activities such as paving surfaces and groundwater pumping significantly alter this balance by impa","topics": [
    "Surface runoff generation mechanisms",
    "Watershed dynamics and storage features",
    "Water budget components and timescales",
    "Human impacts on hydrological balance"
  ]
}

